@node Introduction, Installing Packages, Copying, Top
@chapter Introduction

The ELPA client lets XEmacs consume packages published in the GNU ELPA
format, from GNU ELPA and from MELPA: it downloads an archive index,
resolves a package by name, installs its files into a versioned content
directory under @file{~/.emacs.d/elpa/}, generates autoloads,
byte-compiles the source, and activates the package by adding it to
@code{load-path} and loading its autoloads.  After activation the
package works like any other Lisp library on the system.

The client is deliberately minimal.  It supports @emph{installing} a
small, explicitly allowed set of packages.  It consumes prebuilt
tarballs (and GNU ELPA's single-file artifacts) only: it never builds
from MELPA recipes or package source repositories.  It does not provide
a package menu, browsing, deletion, rollback, or signature
verification.  The old XEmacs package system (@file{package-get.el} and
friends) is unrelated: it has its own index format and download path,
and the two coexist without interacting.

The milestone target set is nine dependency-free MELPA packages plus
the two GNU ELPA packages documented below.  All nine MELPA targets
install and run from unmodified upstream tarballs with no new
compatibility code; this is the @dfn{zero-shim} policy.  The target set
is a support contract, not a user-facing restriction: users may extend
the allowlist (see @ref{Customization} and @ref{Security}).

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .75
@headitem Package @tab Role
@item @code{loop} @tab control flow
@item @code{levenshtein} @tab edit distance; the live-smoke pick
@item @code{csv} @tab CSV parsing
@item @code{vector-utils} @tab vector manipulation
@item @code{number} @tab number parsing and formatting
@item @code{rings} @tab buffer rings
@item @code{shell-split-string} @tab shell-like string splitting
@item @code{kv} @tab key/value data structures
@item @code{unidecode} @tab Unicode transliteration; declares
@code{lexical-binding: t} yet runs unchanged
@end multitable

@code{queue} (a @code{tar} package) and @code{loc-changes} (a
@code{single}-file package) exercise the GNU ELPA path.  All targets
install and run in a fresh XEmacs with no dependencies beyond core.

