@node Refreshing the Index
@section Refreshing the Index

An @dfn{archive} publishes an index file @file{archive-contents}
listing everything it offers.  @code{elpa-install} refreshes the
selected archive's index before resolving; you can also refresh it
explicitly:

@deffn Command elpa-refresh-contents &optional archive
Fetch, validate, cache, and parse the index of @var{archive}, an
archive name from @code{elpa-archives}; the first configured archive
when omitted.  Return the number of packages the index lists.  The
index must carry archive version 1; anything else is rejected.  A local
directory location is read as a file; an HTTP(S) location is fetched
over the network.  @code{elpa-install} performs this refresh itself
before resolving, so an explicit refresh is only needed to inspect an
index or to force a cached copy up to date.
@end deffn

@node Installing a Package
@section Installing a Package

@deffn Command elpa-install name &optional version
Install the package @var{name} (a symbol or string) from the first
archive in @code{elpa-archives}.  The name must appear in
@code{elpa-installable-packages}; anything else is refused.

Install always refreshes the archive index before resolving.  With no
@var{version}, this is a @dfn{floating install}: the client installs
whatever build the archive currently advertises, which is what a
rolling-release archive such as MELPA requires.  A cached index is
never trusted for a rolling archive, because old artifacts disappear
and a stale cache can point at a dead download.  When @var{version} is
given, the archive must still advertise exactly that version; a version
the archive has pruned signals that the version is no longer available
in that archive.  Returns @var{name} on success.

Installing a newer build replaces the older one in place.  The client
publishes the new content directory and only then deletes the old
build, so a failed replacement leaves the old build intact, and after a
successful one exactly one active content directory remains for the
package.  Installation is all-or-nothing: the download lands in a
staging directory and the content directory is published by an atomic
rename only after validation, autoload generation, and byte-compilation
have all succeeded; any failure removes the staging residue and leaves
no partial package directory behind.
@end deffn

@defun elpa-installed-p name &optional version
Return non-@code{nil} if the content directory for @var{name} (at
@var{version}, when given) exists under @code{elpa-user-dir}.  Without
@var{version}, the query keys off the newest installed build, not the
first directory encountered, so it reflects what actually runs.
@end defun

@defun elpa-package-info name &optional archive
Return the plist describing @var{name} as its archive advertises it,
with properties @code{:name}, @code{:version}, @code{:reqs},
@code{:summary}, @code{:kind} (@code{tar} or @code{single}), and
@code{:archive}.
@end defun

@node Activation at Startup
@section Activation at Startup

@deffn Command elpa-activate-all
Add every installed package's content directory to @code{load-path}
and load its generated autoloads file.  Idempotent: directories and
autoloads already activated are skipped.  Entries that are not package
directories (archives, staging) are ignored.
@end deffn

At startup XEmacs calls @code{elpa-activate-all}
automatically when @code{elpa-user-dir} exists and
@code{elpa-activate-at-startup} is non-@code{nil}, so packages installed
with autoload cookies are callable without an explicit
@code{require}.

@node Customization, Security, Installing Packages, Top
@chapter Customization

@defopt elpa-archives
The archives the client knows, as an alist of
@code{(@var{name} . @var{location})}.  The default is GNU ELPA only:
@code{(("gnu" . "https://elpa.gnu.org/packages/"))}.  MELPA is
supported but not configured by default; add an entry such as
@code{("melpa" . "https://melpa.org/packages/")} to use it.  A location
is either an @code{http}s URL or a local directory path (useful for
testing and air-gapped installs).  Archive configuration selects a
source; it does not grant trust (@pxref{Security}).
@end defopt

@defopt elpa-user-dir
The directory packages are installed into,
@file{~/.emacs.d/elpa/} by default.  Created on demand.  Package
content lives in @code{<name>-<version>/} subdirectories; the cached
archive indexes live in @file{archives/@var{name}/}; downloads stage
in @file{.staging/}.
@end defopt

@defopt elpa-installable-packages
The allowlist of packages the client may install, @code{(loop
levenshtein csv vector-utils number rings shell-split-string kv
unidecode queue loc-changes)} by default: the nine MELPA targets and
the two GNU ELPA targets.  This list, not the packages' own declared
compatibility, is the safety boundary: a name not on the list is
refused before any download happens.  Trust is by name and spans all
configured archives.  See @ref{Security}.
@end defopt

@defopt elpa-activate-at-startup
Whether startup runs @code{elpa-activate-all} automatically.  Non-nil
by default; set @code{nil} to keep installed packages inert until
@code{elpa-activate-all} is called explicitly.
@end defopt

@node Security, Data Model, Customization, Top
@chapter Security

This client sits between two kinds of archive.  MELPA is an
@dfn{unsigned-by-design archive}: it publishes no signatures for its
index or its artifacts, as a property of the archive, so allowing it is
a posture rather than a deferred feature.  GNU ELPA is a
@dfn{verification-deferred archive}: it signs its index and packages,
but this client does not perform those checks yet.

The @emph{only} trust controls are HTTPS transport and the flat
per-package allowlist in @code{elpa-installable-packages}.  There is no
signature verification, no checksum pinning, and no TLS pinning.  The
allowlist grants trust by name across all configured archives; it is
not a per-archive permission, because trust and source selection are
different concepts.  The MELPA grant is inert until a MELPA archive is
configured.  The @code{emacs} pseudo-dependency is ignored with a
warning, since XEmacs cannot truthfully claim the declared Emacs
version.

The residual risk is stated verbatim in the client header:

@quotation
Trust model: this client performs no signature verification. MELPA
publishes no signatures, by design; GNU ELPA signature checking is
deferred to a future core effort. The only controls are HTTPS transport
and the `elpa-installable-packages' allowlist: if an archive is
compromised or the connection is tampered with, any allowlisted package
can run arbitrary code. The allowlist is not a cast-iron guarantee —
keep it short, and install only packages you would trust with your
keystrokes.
@end quotation

The archive-shape checks described under @ref{Data Model} are failure
containment, not authentication: a @code{tar} package is validated
before extraction (every member must live under the single top-level
@code{<name>-<version>/} directory; absolute paths, @code{..}
components, and link entries are rejected before @command{tar} runs),
and autoloads files shipped inside a tarball are deleted, never loaded.
They cannot detect a malicious package that bears an allowlisted name.

